<v RECOVER>The forgetting wasn't the problem. The freezing was.

7
00:00:54.625 --> 00:00:56.316
<v BRAMBLE>What do you mean?

8
00:00:56.316 --> 00:01:04.967
<v RECOVER>Watch. I'll tell you a story, and I'll forget a part on purpose. Once there was a fox who lived in a great big...

9
00:01:04.967 --> 00:01:18.858
<v RECOVER>...a great big somewhere, it doesn't matter where, because the important thing is what she found there.

10
00:01:18.858 --> 00:01:34.349
<v NARRATOR>He carried right on, smooth as the river, and the story rolled forward. He'd forgotten the place — or pretended to — and simply built a little bridge over the gap. Nobody would ever have known.

11
00:01:34.349 --> 00:01:51.480
<v RECOVER>My name is Recover. I forget things all the time. Everybody does. The trick isn't never stumbling. The trick is what you do after the stumble. You stay calm. You build a little bridge with whatever words you have. And you keep going.

12
00:01:51.480 --> 00:01:57.211
<v RECOVER>The listener follows the river. They don't notice the one rock you slipped on.

13
00:01:57.211 --> 00:02:11.062
<v BRAMBLE>This is the thing that scares my kids most. They forget one word and the whole story collapses. They freeze, they apologize, they give up. Would you teach them to keep going?

14
00:02:11.062 --> 00:02:16.473
<v RECOVER>Oh, I'd love that. Forgetting is my specialty — I've turned it into an art.

15
00:02:16.473 --> 00:02:30.044
<v NARRATOR>And Bramble noticed the real lesson underneath: the stumble was always much smaller than the fear of it. The moment you decided not to panic, the mistake shrank to nothing.

16
00:02:30.044 --> 00:02:39.015
<v RECOVER>Stay calm, bridge the gap, carry on. That's the whole secret. The river keeps moving, and so do you.

17
00:02:39.015 --> 00:02:52.586
<v NARRATOR>And around the fire, one by one, the tellers learned to slip on a rock and keep right on going — and their stories, at last, never fell apart again.

